  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A strong system Friday through Saturday is \nexpected to bring widespread rain with some snow over the North \nFriday/Friday night and strong winds Friday night into Saturday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA strong low pressure system is expected to develop over the \nGreat Lakes, then move east towards the state this weekend. This\nsystem is expected to bring widespread precip to the region \nFriday. As of this update, ensemble solutions are gradually \nconverging on a track through the northern half of the state but\nthere are still groupings of members wanting to take the track \na bit south. The updated track of the system closer to the \nnorth will limit colder air making it further south, so snow \nchances have decreased. The track is still relatively favorable \nfor some snowfall in northern areas as winds will have an \neasterly component, preventing substantial warm air from \nadvecting into the area. Most areas that see snow will probably \nsee less than an inch but a few areas near the Canadian border \nmight reach an inch or possibly two. Snow amounts are likely to \nbe terrain dependent with higher elevations receiving the \ngreater amounts. Any areas that do see some snow will transition\nto rain in the afternoon as temperatures warm. Widespread rain \namounts of a third of an inch of rain to half an inch of rain \nare expected for the forecast area. Some slick roads are \npossible Friday night as temperatures drop to around freezing. \nSnow showers in the north are likely to persist into Saturday \nwith strong, gusty winds up to 40 mph. Along with temperatures \nin the 30s this is likely to result in wind chills dipping into \nthe 20s. Isolated power outages are possible with the gusty \nwinds.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Extended dry period from the weekend through at\nleast late next week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nAs the system departs Saturday, high pressure will build in over\nthe area. High pressure is expected to continue to dominate\nthrough next week as the jet stream generally stays to our\nnorth. High pressure will limit precipitation chances through\nnext week and lead to seasonable temperatures with highs\ngenerally in the 50s and lows in the 30s.",
